Swansea has no shortage of web designers, agencies, and freelancers. That makes finding the right one harder, not easier. Here's how to cut through the noise and make a decision you won't regret.

## Start with what you need the website to do

Before you look at a single portfolio, get clear on the outcome you need. Do you want to generate more enquiries? Rank higher on Google? Sell products online? Look more credible to potential clients?

A web designer who understands business goals — not just design — will ask about this early. One who jumps straight into talking about platforms, features, and timelines without understanding your business is a red flag.

## Check their portfolio — and look past the surface

A portfolio tells you a lot, but not everything. Screenshots look good. What you actually need to know is whether those websites work.

When you look at a Swansea web designer's portfolio, ask:

- Do the sites load quickly on mobile?
- Are the calls to action clear — is it obvious what a visitor is supposed to do?
- Do the sites rank in Google for local searches?
- Are these real businesses, or concept projects?

If a designer can show you a Swansea trade business or local service that's generating enquiries from Google, that's worth more than a visually impressive concept piece.

[View the NC Digital portfolio](/portfolio/) to see real websites built for South Wales businesses.

## Ask whether SEO is built in from day one

A website that can't be found on Google is a wasted investment. Local SEO — getting your site to appear when people in Swansea search for your service — needs to be built into the foundation of every website, not added later as an afterthought.

Ask any Swansea web designer:

- Do you include on-page SEO as standard?
- Will my site be set up in Google Search Console?
- What location signals will you include for Swansea and surrounding areas?

At NC Digital, every [Swansea web design](/web-design-swansea/) project includes on-page SEO foundations as standard. If you want to go further, our [Swansea SEO service](/seo-swansea/) builds on that with a dedicated monthly campaign.

## Know who you're actually working with

Some agencies in Swansea have multiple layers between you and the person building your site. Your brief gets passed down, you end up working with an account manager, and the designer you never spoke to makes decisions about your business based on a document.

If direct communication matters — and it should — look for an independent designer or small studio where you work with the person doing the work throughout.

## Red flags to watch for

- **No real portfolio** — if they can't show you live examples, walk away
- **Vague pricing** — you deserve a clear quote before you commit
- **No mention of SEO** — a designer who doesn't talk about search performance isn't thinking about your long-term results
- **Templates sold as custom work** — ask directly: is this built from scratch or adapted from a theme?
- **No post-launch support** — find out exactly what's included after your site goes live

## Ask about hosting, maintenance, and ongoing support

Your website needs reliable hosting and regular maintenance to stay secure and fast. A good web designer will be clear about what happens after launch — whether that's a [hosting and security package](/services/website-hosting-security/), a [maintenance retainer](/services/website-maintenance-packages/), or a handover to your own hosting.

## Consider branding too

If your logo looks dated or inconsistent, a new website won't fix it — it'll just display your weak brand on a better canvas. Some Swansea web designers offer [logo design](/logo-design-swansea/) as part of the project, which means your identity and site are designed to work together from day one.

## The bottom line

The right Swansea web designer will ask the right questions, show you real results, be clear about pricing, and stay available after launch.

